Public Health, Erasmus MC, University Medical Center Rotterdam, PO Box 2040, 3000 CA Rotterdam, The Netherlands e.steyerberg@erasmusmc.nl."}]}}, {"fullName": "Vergouwe Y", "lastName": "Vergouwe", "firstName": "Yvonne", "initials": "Y", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Public Health, Erasmus MC, University Medical Center Rotterdam, PO Box 2040, 3000 CA Rotterdam, The Netherlands."}]}}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "29", "volume": "35", "journalIssueId": 2186546, "dateOfPublication": "2014 Aug", "monthOfPublication": 8, "yearOfPublication": 2014, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Eur Heart J", "title": "European heart journal", "essn": "1522-9645", "issn": "0195-668X", "nlmid": "8006263", "isoabbreviation": "Eur Heart J"}, "printPublicationDate": "2014-08-01"}, "pubYear": "2014", "pageInfo": "1925-1931", "abstractText": "Clinical prediction models provide risk estimates for the presence of disease (diagnosis) or an event in the future course of disease (prognosis) for individual patients. Although publications that present and evaluate such models are becoming more frequent, the methodology is often suboptimal. We propose that seven steps should be considered in developing prediction models: (i) consideration of the research question and initial data inspection; (ii) coding of predictors; (iii) model specification; (iv) model estimation; (v) evaluation of model performance; (vi) internal validation; and (vii) model presentation. The validity of a prediction model is ideally assessed in fully independent data, where we propose four key measures to evaluate model performance: calibration-in-the-large, or the model intercept (A); calibration slope (B); discrimination, with a concordance statistic (C); and clinical usefulness, with decision-curve analysis (D). As an application, we develop and validate prediction models for 30-day mortality in patients with an acute myocardial infarction. This illustrates the usefulness of the proposed framework to strengthen the methodological rigour and quality for prediction models in cardiovascular research.", "affiliation": "Department of Public Health, Erasmus MC, University Medical Center Rotterdam, PO Box 2040, 3000 CA Rotterdam, The Netherlands e.steyerberg@erasmusmc.nl.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "review-article", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NINDS NIH HHS", "acronym": "NS", "grantId": "1U01-NS086294-01", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NINDS NIH HHS", "acronym": "NS", "grantId": "U01 NS086294", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Diagnosis"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Prognosis"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Calibration"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Models, Statistical"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Risk Assessment", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ST", "qualifierName": "standards"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Reproducibility of Results"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Decision Support Techniques"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Clinical Coding", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}]}, "keywordList": {"keyword": ["Discrimination", "Prediction model", "shrinkage", "calibration", "Non-linearity", "Missing Values", "Clinical Usefulness"]}, "subsetList": {"subset": [{"code": "IM", "name": "Index Medicus"}]}, "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availability